Name: sequencesAsLists

Type: property

Syntax: set the sequencesAsLists to {true | false}

Summary:
Specifies whether arrays marked as sequences go into xTalk Builder
as Lists.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux, android, html5

Platforms: desktop, server, mobile

Example:
set the sequencesAsLists to true
set the points of widget "Chart" to {"series": [3, 1, 4]} -- "series" is a List

Example:
-- Copy a widget whose saved state holds Lists
local tState
export widget "Original" to array tState
set the sequencesAsLists to true
import widget from array tState

Value:
The <sequencesAsLists> is true or false. It is false when a <handler>
starts.

Description:
An xTalk Builder List comes into script as an array keyed 1 to N,
marked as a sequence (see <is strictly>). Going back into xTalk
Builder, an array is an Array, whatever is in it, except where a
handler's parameter or a property is itself a List, so a List inside an
Array arrives as an Array.

Set the <sequencesAsLists> to true to have every array marked as a
sequence go into xTalk Builder as a List, however deep it is: to a
xTalk Builder handler's parameters, to a widget's properties, and to
the state that <import> `widget from array` gives to the widget's
`OnLoad` handler. That last is what lets a widget whose saved state
holds Lists be copied with <export> and <import>, as it is when its
stack is saved and opened. An array that is marked as a dictionary, or
has no mark, or whose keys are no longer 1 to N, still goes in as an
Array.

An array is marked as a sequence when it is written `[...]`, converted
with `as a sequence`, or came from a List; see <as>.

Since the <sequencesAsLists> is a local <property>, its value is
reset to false when the current <handler> finishes executing. It
retains its value only for the current <handler>, and setting it in one
<handler> does not affect its value in other <handlers> it calls.

`sequencesAsLists` is not a reserved word: a <variable>, a <handler> or
a <custom property> named `sequencesAsLists` works as it always has,
and `the sequencesAsLists of` an <object> is that object's
<custom property>.

Changes:
Added in OXT-Beyond 0.2.8-dp.3.

References: as (operator), is strictly (operator), export (command),
import (command), handler (glossary), handlers (glossary),
property (glossary), custom property (glossary), variable (glossary),
object (glossary)

Tags: properties
